<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Technology – Lesson # 1</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Pros and Cons</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Receptive: Show comprehension of extended instructions on familiar topics. Interactive: Interpret peer feedback in collaborative projects.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Can follow extended instructions on tech-free routines by organizing a sequence of steps during group work (e.g., "Turn off your phone after school, then do a puzzle, read a chapter from a book, and write in your journal.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can respond to instructions on how to reduce screen time (e.g., "What should we do?" "Read a book instead.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can show comprehension of peer feedback by interpreting key points about technology use (e.g., "What did Anna say about her tech-free day?" "She read a lot.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Warm-up:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher plays two short audio clips. Clip 1 features sounds related to technology (phone ringing, social media notification, keyboard typing). Clip 2 features sounds related to tech-free activities (pages turning, birds chirping, board game pieces moving). Students listen and raise their hand when they identify sounds related to "technology" or "disconnecting" from it. The teacher asks, "What did you hear that was 'technology'?" and "What sounds made you think of 'disconnecting'?" This activates prior knowledge and introduces the theme of pros and cons.</p>

                <b>Modeling:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words while students listen and repeat: <em>technology, cellphone, internet, social media, disconnect, relaxed, focused</em>. The teacher then models sentences integrating grammar and pragmatics: "I think <b>social media</b> is <b>helpful</b>, but it can make me feel <b>distracted</b>." "You <b>should</b> try to <b>disconnect</b> sometimes to feel more <b>relaxed</b>." The teacher also models the pronunciation of words with /θ/ and /ð/ sounds (e.g., "thing," "these") and common past tense verbs with "-ed" endings such as "saved" (/d/), "disconnected" (/ɪd/), and "liked" (/t/) for students to listen and distinguish.</p>

                <b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you 'disconnect' from your cellphone, are you using it?" (Expected answer: No.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is being 'distracted' a good thing when you need to study?" (Expected answer: No, it's not helpful.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Material:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Short audio dialogue (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) between two 8th-grade students, Ana and Leo, discussing their experiences with technology and reducing screen time. The dialogue will include target vocabulary (e.g., <em>device, social media, solution, benefits, use, reduce, interact, focused, distracted</em>) and grammatical structures (modals: "You <b>should</b> try to," "You <b>can</b> read"; comparatives: "It's <b>easier</b> to get distracted," "It's <b>more difficult</b> to focus"; present perfect: "I <b>have spent</b> more time with friends <b>since</b> last week").</p>

                <b>Communicative Competence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will show receptive comprehension of general and specific information in short, clear audio messages and dialogues related to familiar topics, specifically the pros and cons of technology.</p>

                <b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher asks, "What are Ana and Leo talking about in general?" and "What is the main problem they discuss?" (Students listen for the overall topic: technology use and its impact).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and decide if statements are True or False. E.g., "Ana thinks social media is always helpful." (False). "Leo suggests reading a book to reduce screen time." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Information Matching):</b> The teacher provides a list of problems (e.g., feeling distracted) and solutions (e.g., reading a book) mentioned in the dialogue. Students listen and match the problems to the corresponding solutions mentioned by Ana or Leo.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will practice listening for specific details, identifying target vocabulary, grammatical structures (modals, comparatives, present perfect), and pragmatic functions (giving opinions, offering suggestions) in short spoken contexts related to technology pros and cons.</p>

                <b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen & Fill-in-the-Blanks):</b> The teacher plays several short sentences extracted or adapted from the audio dialogue. These sentences will have a blank space where a target vocabulary word (e.g., <em>solution, benefits, use, reduce, distracted, focused</em>) or a modal verb should fit. Students listen and write the missing word(s). Example: "Ana said a good ______ to screen time is to read." "Leo thinks it's ______ to concentrate without his cellphone."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listen & Categorize):</b> The teacher reads aloud short statements or questions. Students listen and categorize them into "Pros of Technology," "Cons of Technology," or "Suggestions." For example: "I think technology is useful because it helps me learn." (Pro). "Digital technology makes communication faster and easier, but it can be distracting." (Pro/Con). "You could try using less screen time." (Suggestion). This also integrates modals and comparatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen & Confirm):</b> In pairs, students take turns reading short sentences or questions from a provided list related to technology use (e.g., "What <b>should</b> I do to <b>reduce</b> my screen time?"). Their partner listens carefully and then confirms understanding by rephrasing or answering briefly, using one of the target vocabulary words or grammatical structures. E.g., Student A: "What did Anna say about her tech-free day?" Student B: "She said she felt more <b>relaxed</b> and <b>focused</b> because she <b>disconnected</b>." This allows for interactive listening and peer feedback interpretation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(12-15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Task Format: Group Tech-Free Routine Challenge Planning</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher gives extended, sequential instructions (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) for planning a 3-hour "Tech-Free Routine" for a weekday afternoon. Instructions will use modals ("You <b>should</b> start by...", "You <b>could</b> include...") and reference target vocabulary. E.g., "First, your group <b>should</b> decide on an activity to replace <b>social media</b>. Then, you <b>can</b> choose a healthy way to <b>interact</b> with family or friends. Finally, think about how this routine <b>could</b> help you feel more <b>focused</b> and <b>relaxed</b>." Students listen, take notes on the instructions, and then collaboratively plan their routine, discussing the pros and cons of their chosen activities. They will also offer and interpret peer suggestions and feedback using target language (e.g., "I think it's <b>easier</b> to do a puzzle than read a long book," "You <b>could</b> add a time to <b>disconnect</b> from your <b>device</b>").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students present their group's "Tech-Free Routine" orally to another group or to the class. During the presentation and Q&A, students demonstrate their comprehension of the initial instructions and interpret peer feedback by responding appropriately to questions or suggestions. For example, when asked, "Why <b>should</b> you include 'reading a chapter' in your routine?", a student might respond, "Because reading <b>can</b> help you feel more <b>focused</b> and less <b>distracted</b> than using a <b>cellphone</b>."</p>

                <b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Followed extended instructions for the routine planning.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interpreted peer feedback and responded appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used target vocabulary (e.g., <em>disconnect, focused, relaxed, solution, benefits</em>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used modals (<em>can, could, should</em>) for suggestions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used comparatives (<em>easier, more difficult</em>) to discuss pros/cons.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and intelligibility of spoken responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active collaboration in group discussion.</li>
                </ul>

                <b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one instruction you heard today that helped you plan your tech-free routine?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one 'pro' or 'con' about technology that you heard and understood today?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and Teacher reflection, and connection to next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the most interesting idea you heard about reducing screen time today? Why?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did you understand the instructions and your classmates' suggestions during the activities? What was challenging?</li>
                </ul>

                <b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Today's listening activities helped students activate prior knowledge and engage with the theme. The audio dialogue provided good authentic input for A2.3 learners. Students demonstrated good comprehension of general ideas, but some struggled with identifying specific vocabulary words during controlled listening tasks. The modeling of pronunciation for /θ/, /ð/ and "-ed" endings was helpful, but more focused practice might be needed. The group planning activity fostered good interactive listening and the use of modals for suggestions. Some groups need more scaffolding on how to give and interpret peer feedback effectively. I need to ensure instructions for group tasks are broken down into even smaller steps and repeated for clarity.</p>

                <b>Connection to next lesson:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a text that builds upon the vocabulary and concepts introduced today regarding the pros and cons of technology and strategies to reduce screen time. The text will reinforce the meaning and context of words like <em>solution, benefits, use, reduce, disconnect, interact, save, helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused</em>, preparing them for deeper comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>